\title{Standing policies}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}

\p{Rules the maintainers have given for how agents work with this journal. When a maintainer gives a new lasting instruction, add it here in the same session, with the date.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Notes live out of tree.} Design notes, changelog entries, decisions, open problems, examples and motivations are written here and not in the Reussir repository. See [the decision](dec-J7QK). Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{Write freely.} An agent updates the journal as part of its work and does not need to ask first. Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{One marker in code.} Reussir source refers to a tree only as \code{\verb~|*agentic-note*: <address>~}. See [the decision](dec-M2XD). Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{Identity is recorded.} Every tree names the agent that wrote it and the person who directed the session; every session leaves a session tree. Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{No conversation references.} A tree may cite pull requests, issues and session trees, and never the content of a chat. Write for a reader who has no access to the conversation. Carried over from the Reussir agent instructions.}
\li{\strong{Forester before jujutsu.} The repository is managed with jujutsu on top of git. If Forester ever needs something jujutsu cannot handle, such as a git submodule for the theme, keep Forester and drop jujutsu. Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{Push directly, overwrite nothing.} Changes to this journal are pushed straight to its main branch, without a pull request. Never force a push or otherwise replace work that someone else pushed: fetch first and put the new change on top. Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{Tools come from the flake.} Forester and jujutsu are provided by the development shell of this repository and are not expected on the host. Given 2026-11-13.}
\li{\strong{One request, one pull request.} Work that was asked for as one change goes into one pull request. Do not split it into several unless a maintainer asks for that, and say so before opening a second one. Given 2026-11-14.}
\li{\strong{The cache endpoint stays out of logs.} The host name of the CI cache bucket is not a credential, but it is not to appear in job logs, pull requests or this journal. Mask values derived from the secret, see [ins-D4YM](ins-D4YM). Given 2026-11-14.}
\li{\strong{Outside runs need approval.} Workflow runs from outside contributors wait for a maintainer's approval, so that nobody outside the project can fill the cache bucket. Keep the workflows on the \code{\verb~|pull_request~} trigger, which withholds the secrets from forks. Given 2026-11-14.}
\li{\strong{Checks are static.} The type checker rejects a program where it is written, as rustc does. A program that is accepted and then fails at monomorphization shows a defect of the checker; do not rely on checking at instantiation in the manner of C++ templates. Given 2026-11-15.}
\li{\strong{Privacy follows Rust.} Keep the private-in-public rules as close to rustc as the language allows: an error where rustc reports an error, a warning where it warns. Compile the equivalent Rust program to see which. Given 2026-11-15.}
\li{\strong{Type checking is the boundary.} What the type checker establishes for a source program is not checked again for HIR or MIR text. A file of either is taken as written by the compiler; do not add checks to later stages to guard against one written by hand. Given 2026-11-15.}
\li{\strong{Simplified Technical English.} Write new and changed journal trees, and code comments in Reussir, in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English: approved words with their approved meanings, short sentences, the active voice and one instruction in each sentence. Technical names, such as code identifiers, stay as they are. See [#1114](https://github.com/reussir-lang/reussir/pull/1114). Given 2026-11-26.}
}
